From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 05:40:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media (ohos): Refuse data that the playback buffer cannot
 take

Once the playback buffer of the OpenHarmony AVPlayer backend was full,
it kept the part of a chunk that fitted and dropped the rest and every
later chunk, while `push_data` reported success. The media element
could not tell which bytes the player had taken, so a refill restarted
the fetch at the end of the buffered bytes with a SeekData event, and
every refill of a fetch without range support downloaded the resource
from its start again.

Refuse a chunk that does not fit whole with `PlayerError::EnoughData`,
like the GStreamer backend, and still send the EnoughData event once.
The media element then keeps the chunk and knows the next byte that the
player expects. Once AVPlayer reads close to the end of the buffered
bytes, accept pushes again and send NeedData: the media element pushes
the chunks it kept, or restarts a fetch it cancelled at that byte, so a
contiguous refill needs neither a seek nor the handshake that waits
for the media element. NeedData is sent with the buffer locked, like
EnoughData, which keeps it ordered before the SeekData of any later
read without relying on the handshake of the seek lock. Reads elsewhere
still restart the fetch with SeekData.

A refill of a fetch without range support now drains the chunks that
the media element queued instead of downloading the resource again,
unless the element's 32 MiB queue cap cancelled that fetch, for example
while paused, which still restarts it from the start until a fetch can
be suspended. So a refill starts before AVPlayer reaches the end of the
buffered bytes for every fetch, and the buffer no longer needs to know
whether the server supports range requests.

The first chunk after NeedData, which is the refused chunk or the first
chunk of the resumed fetch, is taken whole even if it does not fit, as
is a chunk that arrives once AVPlayer read every buffered byte, like
the whole body of a data URL. Refusing it again would only stop the
fetch again, or stall playback. The buffer exceeds its capacity by at
most that chunk, and shrinks back once its bytes fit again.

In the media element, don't warn about a refused chunk, which is now
part of every refill, and update a comment that described the old
behaviour.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: unit tests for the playback buffer, which only build for
OpenHarmony; see Validation.

Validation: the 19 unit tests of playback_buffer.rs, which only build for OpenHarmony, pass when the file is built as a standalone host crate, and each old behaviour makes a test fail when put back (keeping the part of a chunk that fits, restarting the fetch at the end of the buffered bytes instead of resuming it, refilling a fetch without range support only at the end), as do not sending NeedData from within the buffer, refusing the first chunk after NeedData or a chunk for a reader that caught up, not shrinking the buffer, and not ending a resumed fetch. A HiHope DAYU200 (OpenHarmony 7.0, API 26), headless release builds of the series with and without this patch, each scenario in a fresh process, every run played to its end without a stall: a 90 s, 42 MB video without range support takes 1 request and 42.5 MB instead of 5 requests and 212 MB, at the price of up to 30 MB of RSS for the data that the media element queues; a 30 s, 14 MB video without range support takes 3 instead of 5 full downloads (42.8 instead of 71.3 MB) for two passes of `loop`, and 2 instead of 4 (28.5 instead of 57.0 MB) when seeking from 20 s back to 2 s. With range support, every refill still cancels the fetch and starts a new request, so the counts stay the same: 7 requests and 58.5 MB instead of 61.8 and 62.6 MB for the 42 MB video from a local server, 4 requests and 43.0 MB either way at 1.5 MB/s, 4 requests and 34.2 instead of 32.0 MB for the seek. `./mach test-wpt --ohos --processes 1` of the series' 60 media tests: the same results as without this patch, with only the known unexpected OK of autoplay-default-permissions-policy.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; written and tested on a device by the agent, reviewed by another agent)
